# C# / .NET

## Naming

- PascalCase for types, methods, and public members.
- camelCase for locals; `_camelCase` for private fields.
- Prefix interfaces with `I`.
- Prefer meaningful names over abbreviations.
- Async methods end with `Async`.

## Language

- Prefer file-scoped namespaces.
- Prefer `var` when the type is obvious.
- Prefer expression-bodied members when they improve clarity.
- Use modern C# features when they improve readability, safety, or performance.
- Nullable reference types are enabled — honor nullability.

## Async

- Use async/await for asynchronous operations.
- Avoid `.Result` and `.Wait()`.
- Do not wrap async APIs in sync wrappers without a valid reason.

## Dependency injection

- Prefer constructor injection over static dependencies.
- Keep dependencies explicit.
- Avoid service locator patterns.

## Exceptions

- Do not silently catch exceptions.
- Add useful context when rethrowing.
- Use custom exceptions only when they provide meaningful value.

## Quality

- Write readable, self-explanatory code.
- Avoid premature optimization and clever solutions that hurt maintainability.
- Keep methods and classes focused on one responsibility.
- Remove dead code instead of commenting it out.
- Comments explain *why*, complex rules, or non-obvious decisions — not obvious code.
